[
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm trying to set up a proof that a non-Abelian group of order 6 is isomorphic to the permutation group on 3 elements.</p>\n<p>The strategy is to consider the maximum order among all elements in the group. I've run into two problems:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>What I'd really like is to use a proof that the maximum order belongs to <code>Nat.divisors 6</code> to examine only those four cases. I believe that the tactic for this is supposed to be <code>fin_cases</code>. However, in my code, <code>fin_cases</code> throws an error saying <code>Dependent elimination failed</code>; I don't understand why.</li>\n<li>As a workaround, I've also proved that the maximum order is a natural number no greater than 6. This means I can use <code>interval_cases</code> to examine seven cases. This works, in theory. However, it ends up rewriting the connection between the variable defined to be the maximum order, and the definition of that variable as the maximum order, making it difficult for me to conclude that any other group element must have an order no larger than the maximum. Can I stop <code>interval_cases</code> from rewriting certain hypotheses?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Here's the example:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">GroupTheory</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">SpecificGroups</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Dihedral</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- When G is a nonabelian group of order 6, G ≃ S₃.</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Group</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h_order6</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">card</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h_nonabelian</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≃*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Equiv</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Perm</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"o\">),</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G_fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finite</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">finite_of_card_ne_zero</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">h_order6</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">trivial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- 6 ≠ 0</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- For typing reasons, we're going to use a `Finset` containing all of G.</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fintype</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fintype</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ofFinite</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">set_G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">univ</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">set_G</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">card</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">card_univ</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fintype</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">card_eq_nat_card</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h_order6</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- Consider the set of all orders of every element of G</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">orders</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">image</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">orderOf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">set_G</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">orders</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Nonempty</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Nonempty</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">image</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">univ_nonempty</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- One of those orders is the highest:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">max_order_in_G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">max'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">orders</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h3</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h4</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">max_order_in_G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">orders</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">max'_mem</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mem_image</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h4</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">obtain</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ha</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h4</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- And since the order of the group is 6, the maximum order must divide 6</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h5</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">max_order_in_G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∣</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">←</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h_order6</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">←</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ha</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">orderOf_dvd_natCard</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">--</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- h'5 is what we *want* to do cases on, but `fin_cases` fails for cryptic reasons</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h'5</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">max_order_in_G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">divisors</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mem_divisors</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">And</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h5</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">trivial</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- fin_cases h'5</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h7</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">max_order_in_G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">le_of_dvd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h5</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">trivial</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">interval_cases</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">max_order_in_G</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">norm_num</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h5</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- case 0; not a divisor of 6</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- where the maximum order is 1, G has only one element</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h01</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">orderOf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">le_max'</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h02</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">orderOf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">orders</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mem_image_of_mem</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mem_univ</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h03</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">le_max'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">orders</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">orderOf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h02</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">--</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">-- h03 should now be able to prove that `orderOf x ≤ orderOf a`...</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\">-- ...but the proof that `orderOf a` is `orders.max'` has been eaten by interval_cases</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">done</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">done</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 611942124,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael",
        "timestamp": 1784653595
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>if you <code>import Mathlib.Tactic.Simproc.Divisors</code> you should be able to <code>simp only [Nat.divisors_ofNat] at h'5</code></p>",
        "id": 611946846,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1784655076
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Do you know what's going wrong with <code>Nat.divisors 6</code>? The docstring for <code>Nat.divisors_ofNat</code> says it \"computes the finset <code>Nat.divisors n</code> when <code>n</code> is a numeral\", but I thought that's what <code>Nat.divisors n</code> did too.</p>",
        "id": 611954522,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael",
        "timestamp": 1784657110
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The difference is that <code>Nat.divisors</code> is an ordinary function, but <code>Nat.divisors_ofNat</code> is a simproc (simplification procedure) that instructs the <code>simp</code> tactic on how to turn <code>Nat.divisors 6</code> into <code>{1, 2, 3, 6}</code> when simplifying expressions.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- info: Nat.divisors (n : ℕ) : Finset ℕ -/</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_msgs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">divisors</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- info: Nat.divisors_ofNat : Lean.Meta.Simp.DSimproc -/</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_msgs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">divisors_ofNat</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 611955262,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1784657327
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(Also, <code>simp only [Nat.divisors_ofNat] at h'5</code> still causes <code>fin_cases</code> to fail; it rewrites <code>h'5</code> as <code>max_order_in_G ∈ {1, 2, 3, 6}</code> where the set is a Finset. But if you let <code>simp</code> go unrestricted it rewrites further to <code>max_order_in_G = 1 ∨ max_order_in_G = 2 ∨ max_order_in_G = 3 ∨ max_order_in_G = 6</code>, at which point I assume rcases will do the job.)</p>",
        "id": 611955430,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael",
        "timestamp": 1784657380
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p>The difference is that <code>Nat.divisors</code> is an ordinary function [...]</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Does that matter to <code>fin_cases</code>? I thought <code>fin_cases</code> was already supposed to know how to do that very thing. And it seems like it might not, since <code>fin_cases</code> is still broken after the simp?</p>",
        "id": 611955646,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael",
        "timestamp": 1784657457
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>the implementation of <code>fin_cases</code> isn't very smart, so sometimes it won't be able to do what you want it to do</p>",
        "id": 611955784,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1784657501
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Breaking the hypothesis <code>x ∈ {1, 2, 3, 6}</code> into cases seems like the absolute simplest thing I could ask it to do... <span aria-label=\"speechless\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f636\" role=\"img\" title=\"speechless\">:speechless:</span></p>",
        "id": 611955981,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael",
        "timestamp": 1784657567
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>in this case the reason it doesn't work is because <code>max_order_in_G</code> is a <code>let</code>-bound variable</p>",
        "id": 611956444,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1784657708
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Just to flag that I had some undergraduates classify groups of order &lt; 32 recently, their argument for 6 is here <a href=\"https://github.com/2540825244/m2r-group-7/blob/4044902e4f20670109f0d19ee97c8b3d2c0a239b/M2rGroup7/PqCase.lean#L26\">https://github.com/2540825244/m2r-group-7/blob/4044902e4f20670109f0d19ee97c8b3d2c0a239b/M2rGroup7/PqCase.lean#L26</a> but the guts of the argument is to use the main result of another repo <a href=\"https://github.com/2540825244/order-p-q\">https://github.com/2540825244/order-p-q</a> which classifies groups of order <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">pq</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">pq</span></span></span></span>.</p>",
        "id": 611987551,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1784668031
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Probably <code>have _ : Fintype G := Fintype.ofFinite G</code> should be <code>let ...</code> because <code>Fintype</code> is data (it's an actual list, not just a claim of finiteness).</p>",
        "id": 611987874,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1784668191
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/difficulty.20with.20fin_cases.20and.20interval_cases/near/611987874\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Probably <code>have _ : Fintype G := Fintype.ofFinite G</code> should be <code>let ...</code> because <code>Fintype</code> is data (it's an actual list, not just a claim of finiteness).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>In this case it's probably better to keep it as a <code>have</code> because <code>Fintype.ofFinite</code> isn't actually constructing any data (it's just <code>Classical.choice</code>), and you should never encounter a situation in which it's better to have <code>Fintype.ofFinite</code> in your goal instead of a free variable from the local context.</p>",
        "id": 611988316,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1784668360
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>just make sure you aren't ever able to get a <code>Fintype</code> instance from anywhere else</p>",
        "id": 611988395,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1784668390
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/difficulty.20with.20fin_cases.20and.20interval_cases/near/611987551\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Just to flag that I had some undergraduates classify groups of order &lt; 32 recently, their argument for 6 is here <a href=\"https://github.com/2540825244/m2r-group-7/blob/4044902e4f20670109f0d19ee97c8b3d2c0a239b/M2rGroup7/PqCase.lean#L26\">https://github.com/2540825244/m2r-group-7/blob/4044902e4f20670109f0d19ee97c8b3d2c0a239b/M2rGroup7/PqCase.lean#L26</a> but the guts of the argument is to use the main result of another repo <a href=\"https://github.com/2540825244/order-p-q\">https://github.com/2540825244/order-p-q</a> which classifies groups of order <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">pq</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">pq</span></span></span></span>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Mathlib has a classification of groups of squarefree order btw:<br>\n<a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=IsZGroup.of_squarefree#doc\">docs#IsZGroup.of_squarefree</a> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=isZGroup_iff_exists_mulEquiv#doc\">docs#isZGroup_iff_exists_mulEquiv</a></p>",
        "id": 611988499,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1784668447
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I thought I'd mention that when I have <code>h1 : orderOf g ∈ Nat.divisors 6</code> <strong>and</strong> <code>h2 : orderOf g ≤ 2</code>, using <code>simp [Nat.divisors_ofNat, h2] at h1</code> produces cases for all four divisors of 6, ignoring the hypothesis that they have to be at most 2.</p>\n<p>This isn't blocking me or anything, but it is annoying and it's the kind of thing I'd expect simplification to be able to deal with. (Can it?)</p>\n<p>The same thing happens if I try <code>simp [Nat.divisors_ofNat] at h4</code> against <code>h4 : orderOf g ∈ (Nat.divisors 6).filter (· ≤ 2)</code>, where theoretically <code>3</code> and <code>6</code> aren't even in the Finset that contains <code>orderOf g</code>.</p>",
        "id": 612151896,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael",
        "timestamp": 1784731255
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><code>simp</code> is just a tool which rewrites theorems of the form  <code>A=B</code> or <code>P &lt;-&gt; Q</code>. It won't do most numerical inequalities like <code>6&lt;=2 -&gt; False</code>, that's a different kind of question. Did you try <code>norm_num</code> or <code>decide</code> or <code>linarith</code>? They will work with inequalities. <code>simp</code> doesn't mean \"do an arbitrary simple thing\".</p>",
        "id": 612206523,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1784745992
    }
]